= Foreword

Health technology assessment sits at an uncomfortable junction: the
discipline demands the statistical rigour of a biostatistics
department, the structural clarity of an actuarial model, and the
practical honesty of a budget-holder who has to live with the
decision an analysis recommends. Few analysts arrive at HEOR already
fluent in all three. Most arrive, as this book's author did, from a
single adjacent discipline — public health, epidemiology, clinical
medicine, economics, or data science — and build the rest on the job,
one model, one submission, one committee critique at a time.

This book exists because that on-the-job build is harder than it
should be. The canonical references on health economic modelling
(Briggs, Claxton and Sculpher's *Decision Modelling for Health
Economic Evaluation*; Drummond and colleagues' *Methods for the
Economic Evaluation of Health Care Programmes*) are rightly
foundational, but they were not written as R programming references,
and R's own documentation was not written with HTA submission
requirements in mind. The gap between "I understand what a
partitioned survival model is" and "I can build one in R that a NICE
evidence review group will accept without a technical query" is
where most working analysts spend their first several difficult
years.

What follows is an attempt to close that gap directly: every
statistical and economic concept is paired with working R code; every
modelling chapter is grounded in what the major HTA agencies actually
ask to see; every method is introduced with the assumption that the
reader is capable and motivated, not that the reader has already sat
through a two-year health economics master's programme. Readers
coming from applied public health and programmatic data work — TB
programme analysis, national disease registries, LMIC health systems
— will find this book written with their vantage point as an
explicit first-class concern, not an afterthought bolted onto a
Western-payer-market default.

No single book makes anyone a health economist. This one aims to make
the R side of the job considerably less lonely.
